# NEVER commit real evidence, decrypted data, or credentials
evidence/
analysis/
notes/
STATE.md
CONTINUE.md
tools/reconnect/node_modules/
reconnect_creds.json
noise_keys.json
*.pkl
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Runtime secrets/outputs — un-anchored so they match at the repo root TOO, not just
# under tools/reconnect/. The frozen .exe writes these into the CURRENT directory
# (creds_in.json = Noise private key; companion_enc_static.txt = the at-rest master key),
# so a `git add .` from a repo clone must never be able to stage them.
creds_in.json
# Wildcarded like the neighbours: _find_key searches the cwd and every --out directory for this
# name and cmd_reconnect writes it there, so a .bak or dated copy is trivially created and an
# exact-name entry would let it through. This is the at-rest master key.
companion_enc_static.txt*
signal_state.json
appstate_keys.json
# Un-anchored AND wildcarded: the overlay is key material, and archived/renamed copies of it
# (signal_runtime.json.dev43, .bak.dev43, .2026-10-01 ...) must not slip past this the way an
# exact-name list lets them.
signal_runtime.json*
signal_state.json*
appstate_keys.json*
creds_in.json*
anchors.json
notifs.json
history_*.json
# Any file holding RECOVERED MESSAGES is evidence, whatever it is called. The list above names
# outputs one by one, so each new diagnostic that writes recovered data needs a new line and the
# next one gets forgotten -- onehistory_recovered.json was committable for exactly that reason.
# Match on the naming instead of the filename.
*recovered*.json
# Decrypted history chunks: the primary artifact a --history run downloads, kept so a corrected
# mapper can re-read them offline. They ARE message content, so they never go near a commit.
history_chunks/
*_recovered.json
onehistory_recovered.json

# WAInsight is a SEPARATE MIT repo — do NOT bundle it
WAInsight/
wainsight_case/
storage_export/
wa_decrypted/

# THE DECRYPTED OUTPUTS THEMSELVES. `--out` defaults to ./wawf_out, and `--out .` is a thing people
# type, so these land in the current directory -- which, in a clone, is the repo. transcripts.txt and
# chats.json are the message bodies; identities.json is the most directly identifying artifact the
# tool writes (every contact's name, phone number and lid<->phone mapping). Wildcarded like the
# neighbours above, because a dated or .bak copy is trivially made and an exact-name entry lets it by.
wawf_out/
transcripts.txt*
chats.json*
identities.json*

# Chain-of-custody case databases (--audit) and common output dirs: these hold real evidence
# (decrypted messages, contacts, evidence hashes, key fingerprints) and must never be committed.
wawf_audit.db
wawf_audit.db-wal
wawf_audit.db-shm
*.audit.db
db/
out/

# PyInstaller build artifacts (the recipe wawf.spec IS committed; the outputs are not)
build/
dist/
*.log

# reconnect runtime logs/outputs (secrets themselves are covered un-anchored above)
tools/reconnect/baileys.log
wa_sqlite/

# Media recovery working files. media_retry_in.json carries mediaKeys (secrets) and chat jids (PII);
# media_retry_out.json carries freshly minted CDN capability URLs. Un-anchored, like the others.
media_retry_in.json
media_retry_out.json

# the tool's OWN linked-device credentials (pair.js) — key material
wawf_device/

# Everything a history run keeps per linked device (credentials, its own Signal state, the ledger, what
# the server delivered) lives in tools/reconnect/devices/<account>-<device>/. The Python client's
# runtime Signal state is signal_runtime.PY.json, which `signal_runtime.json*` above does not match,
# and its journal of every stanza the server delivered was not covered at all: both sat in
# tools/reconnect/ one `git add .` away from a commit.
devices/
signal_runtime*.json*
*_journal.jsonl*
history_metadata.json*
# a write interrupted half-way leaves its temporary copy (the ledger's holds recovered messages), and
# the .exe keeps history beside itself when no checkout is around it
*.json.tmp
wawf-history/
# --queue without --out writes here; it holds the device's credentials and the decrypted queue
server-queue/
# ...and with --chat, beside the export folder (`<folder>-server-queue/`), so the scoped export holds no other chat
*-server-queue/
queue_*.json*
placeholder_resend.json*

# The rest of an export, for an --out pointed at a clone (`--out .`, `--out sqlite:.`): the contact
# list, the Android databases and recovered media, and the receipts naming what is in them.
contacts.csv*
msgstore.db*
wa.db*
Media/
wawf_export.json
_manifest.json
sessions.json
abprops.json
